The basic idea of Selection Sort is to repeatedly find the smallest element from the unsorted portion of an array and swap it to the front

Time Complexity: O(n²) because it uses two nested loops, meaning it is slow for large datasets.(All cases)
Space Complexity: O(1) auxiliary space because it sorts the array in-place without needing extra memory.
Swaps: It minimizes memory writes, making a maximum of n-1 swaps.


class Solution {
    void selectionSort(int[] arr) {
        // code here
        for(int i=0;i<arr.length-1;i++){
            int min = i;
            for(int j=i+1;j<arr.length;j++){
                if(arr[j] < arr[min]){
                    //assign min index
                    min = j;
                }
            }
            
            //swap
            int temp = arr[i];
            arr[i] = arr[min];
            arr[min] = temp;
        }        
    }
}


